# Arkride Web — copy to .env.local and fill in.
#
# .env.local is gitignored. Never commit real values.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Arkride API
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_. Only Server Components, Server Actions
# and Route Handlers talk to the API, so the browser never needs this value —
# and keeping it server-side means the internal API host is not published in the
# JavaScript bundle.
#
# Local development, matching the backend's PORT:
ARKRIDE_API_URL=http://localhost:4010

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Privy (optional)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The app id is a PUBLIC client identifier and belongs in the bundle — the Privy
# SDK runs in the browser. Leave it unset and Privy sign-in disappears cleanly;
# password sign-in is unaffected.
#
# There is deliberately no PRIVY_APP_SECRET here. This app never verifies a
# Privy token itself: it hands the token to the Arkride API, which verifies it
# against Privy's public key. The secret belongs only to the backend.
#
# Note that the origin you serve this app from must be listed in the Privy
# dashboard's allowed domains, or the SDK will refuse to initialise.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_APP_ID=

# Ark Ride's X profile, linked from the waitlist footer. Leave empty and the
# X badge renders without a link — the design draws it but names no account.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ARKRIDE_X_URL=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostHog analytics (optional)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY unset and no analytics load and no request is
# made. The key is the project TOKEN (starts phc_) — a public identifier that
# belongs in the bundle, like the Privy app id above. It is NOT the personal API
# key the backend's flag sync uses; never put that one here.
#
# Events go to this app's own /ingest path, which next.config.ts forwards to
# PostHog, so the CSP needs no change and ad-blockers do not undercount sign-ups.
# Both variables are read at BUILD time (the Dockerfile takes them as build args).
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=

# Where /ingest is forwarded. Defaults to PostHog cloud US; use
# https://eu.i.posthog.com for an EU project. The `.i.` ingestion host, not
# the app host you log in to.
# POSTHOG_INGEST_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com
